# elohim/elohim-storage/src — Rust storage implementation

HTTP handlers, EPR/blob resolution, P2P dataplane, views, and service modules for the
elohim-storage Rust crate. Editing conventions are in `elohim/elohim-storage/CLAUDE.md`.

## EPR/blob resolution invariant (gate-enforced)

**Peers are the store; doorways are projections with peer-fallback.** The EPR/blob
resolution path (`http.rs` app-resolution handlers, `sharding.rs`, `shard_service.rs`,
`epr_head.rs`, `epr_service.rs`, `epr_atom_service.rs`) MUST never return a hard
"App not found" 404 for a known-EPR-head / missing-blob case without a peer-fallback
branch or an EPR-head-aware syncing-status response.

**The shortcut lives at `http.rs:5589` and `:5601`.** The live failure it caused
(alpha-B, 2026-06-29): the `blobHash` pointer was null while the blob bytes were
present locally — the resolver hard-failed instead of healing from a peer.

The `peer-fallback-invariant` inject rule fires as an advisory (class stays `inject`
because `epr:validator-epr-peer-fallback` is not yet registered in v1) when any `.rs`
write in this directory contains "App not found" or "App ZIP not available". It catches
reintroduction of the shortcut pattern at edit time without blocking legitimate edits.

**The fix landed** (T17): `get_blob_or_heal` (`http.rs`, `BlobHealOutcome`) now runs the
shared local-read + peer-heal sequence behind BOTH `GET /blob/{hash}` and the `/apps/`
resolver, finalizing via `blob_fetch::finalize_fetch_success` (atomic inventory+event
pair). The invariant now guards REGRESSION: new NotFound arms must route through that
shared sequence rather than re-introducing a bare 404. (Historical shortcut lived at
http.rs:5589/5601, alpha-B 2026-06-29; pointer propagation is the CRDT plane, landed
2026-07-02.)

See: `genesis/data/timeline/backlog/dataplane-peer-fallback-and-blob-replication.md`

## Heal invariant — fills-never-moves (gate-surfaced)

**A self-healing loop with a weaker information source than the state it overwrites converts every
restart into a rollback.** The projection-reconcile heal (`src/p2p/projection_reconcile.rs`) and the
stamp path (`src/db/content_diesel.rs` — `stamp_declared_head`, `stamp_declared_head_mode`,
`StampMode`) must obey: heal FILLS absence, it never MOVES authority. `StampMode::Declare` is only
for a **canonical** answer — the zome now returns `canonical: bool` (a coordinator-only, hot-swapped
change); a fallback resolve (`canonical == false`, e.g. a cold conductor falling through to the
root-author election while the canonical link is not yet retrievable) MUST stamp `GapFill`, which may
fill an empty row but never displaces a declared/adopted row.

The live failure (defect class #2, adam-alpha, 2026-07-11): two minutes after the first-ever
cross-conductor adoption, the restarted node's heal restamped the OLD head over the adopted canonical
row — for 2,838 divergence-queued rows, serially, after every restart. The blind first fix (defect
class #3) keyed the guard on *who* writes and froze forward adoption; the cure was making the SOURCE
name the authority it carries (`canonical`), not gating on the writer.

The `heal-fills-never-moves` rule fires as an advisory (class stays `inject` because
`epr:validator-heal-fills-never-moves` is not registered in v1) when any `.rs` write in this subtree
touches the stamp/heal path. It surfaces the invariant at edit time without blocking legitimate edits.

See: `genesis/docs/content/elohim-protocol/history/2026-07-12-substrate-convergence-five-defect-arc.md`

## Governance note

This manifest records two gate-surfaced invariants (peer-fallback on the resolution path;
fills-never-moves on the heal path). No general authoring gate is warranted for the rest of the
directory (operator WIP is active; other editing conventions live in `CLAUDE.md`). Each inject
advisory is the lightest signal that surfaces its lesson where the drift recurs.
